<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nathaniel Isaacs</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1808">1808</a>–<a href="/years/1872">1872</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as uDambuza</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nathaniel Isaacs was an English adventurer, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> into a Jewish family, who played an important part in the history of Natal. Stranded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Port Natal</a> in 1825 when his ship foundered, he lived among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a>, met King Shaka regularly and fought in the ranks of his warriors, and was rewarded with land near present-day Durban and made an induna, a headman, under Shaka. His Travels and Adventures in Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> (1836), whose accuracy is often disputed, became a principal source for the history of Natal. He left in 1831, later built a business from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> and ruled Matakong Island, and in 1854 was accused of slave-trading but escaped prosecution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nathaniel-isaacs.jpg" alt="Nathaniel Isaacs" width="190" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nathaniel-isaacs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Isaacs's father was a shoemaker, and his mother, Lenie (Elizabeth) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, was the daughter of Nathaniel Solomon of Margate and Phoebe Mitz, who came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Some years after his father's death, Isaacs sailed in 1822 to join his maternal uncle Saul Solomon Sr., a merchant at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> on St. Helena; his cousin Saul Solomon Jr. later became an influential liberal politician of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>. In June 1825 he persuaded his uncle to let him sail with James King, captain of the brig Mary, which went on from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Port Natal</a> to look for the merchant-adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-farewell">Francis Farewell</a> and his party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Mary left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> on 26 August 1825 and was wrecked on a sandbank during a gale on entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Port Natal</a>. On 29 November 1825 Isaacs went with King to visit Shaka, who had supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> to the Europeans on his coast. Isaacs learned isiZulu, was wounded fighting for Shaka, and received the praise name uDambuza mtabate, off he goes at speed. With King, Farewell and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-francis-fynn">Henry Francis Fynn</a> he founded the settlement that grew into Durban. He left when Shaka's successor and assassin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a>, was thought to be planning to kill the Europeans on the edge of his kingdom.</p><p>In 1844 Isaacs gave up his claim to the land Shaka had granted him. On Matakong Island he built a port, a private army and a commercial base, ruling with the consent of local kings. When Governor Sir Arthur Kennedy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> accused him of slave-trading, an informant in the governor's secretariat warned him, and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The sworn testimony was lost when the Forerunner, carrying Kennedy, was wrecked off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a> in October 1854, and the English courts declined to proceed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Isaacs spent his last years with a daughter's family near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> and died on 26 June 1872 at Egremont, across the Mersey; he was buried in the Canterbury Jewish Cemetery. His book appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> within a decade of Shaka's death, while Fynn's reminiscences were not published until 1950, so it did much to shape the image of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> king. Dan Wylie of Rhodes University argued that Isaacs exaggerated Shaka's brutality to sell books, and Stephen Gray also doubted him. Adam Rovner of the University of Denver, whose biography appeared in 2025, concluded that the archival record largely supports his claims.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nathaniel Isaacs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Much of what is known comes from European traders such as <b>Nathaniel Isaacs</b> and Henry Francis Fynn, whose accounts historians now read with caution, and from the Zulu oral histories James Stuart collected around 1900.</blockquote><a href="/societies/zulu-kingdom">Social Hierarchy of the Zulu Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.28,1.08&amp;year=1849" aria-label="Canterbury on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4574 387 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nathaniel Isaacs"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5024 657h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5024 657h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.28,1.08&amp;year=1849">What was here in 1849? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
